<node id="OLD HEN">
  <data key="d0">OLD HEN</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">The 'old hen' is a metaphorical figure in the text who takes charge of planning and guiding a group of travelers. She is portrayed as cautious, organized, and determined to ensure a safe and enjoyable journey for everyone. Her role is central to the narrative as she sets the rules and expectations for the trip. Key attributes include her leadership, caution, and strong sense of responsibility. She has a relationship with the 'chickens,' whom she is trying to guide. Historically, this term may refer to a traditional or archaic way of referring to a motherly figure. Notable actions include setting rules and leading the group on a journey.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 34}]</data>
</node>
<node id="JACK THE GIANTKILLER">
  <data key="d0">JACK THE GIANTKILLER</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Jack the Giantkiller is a mythical figure from Cornish folklore, representing courage and the triumph of good over evil. He is mentioned as an early influence on the author's imagination. Key attributes include his mythological status and association with Cornish culture. He has a relationship with King Arthur, both being seen as symbols of justice and bravery. Historically, he is part of Cornish mythology and represents the spirit of the region. Notable actions include his legendary battles against giants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 28}]</data>
</node>
<node id="KING ARTHUR">
  <data key="d0">KING ARTHUR</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">King Arthur is a legendary figure associated with Tintagel and the surrounding area. He is described as the 'stainless king' and is linked to the ruins and the stories of the past. The text suggests that the site may have been the landing-place of King Arthur or other Cornish heroes, reinforcing its mythological significance.&lt;SEP&gt;King Arthur is a legendary figure in British history and mythology, known for leading the defense of Britain against Saxon invaders. He is central to many medieval tales and is often associated with the concept of chivalry and honor. His grave is a significant historical and cultural landmark in the region described. King Arthur is also a symbol of idealized leadership and heroism in literature.&lt;SEP&gt;King Arthur is a legendary figure in British history and mythology, often associated with the Arthurian legends. In this context, his soul is believed to revisit familiar scenes, indicating a deep cultural and historical significance. He is a central figure in the narrative, symbolizing the connection between the past and present. His presence is tied to the locations mentioned, such as Tintagel and Porth Hern, which are historically significant sites. There are no notable actions or events directly attributed to him in the text, but his legend influences the atmosphere of the place.&lt;SEP&gt;King Arthur is a legendary figure in British history and mythology, often associated with the Arthurian legends. In this text, he is referenced through Tennyson's depiction of him as an ideal knight and the belief that the type of man he represents may still exist in Cornwall. The text also connects him to the Cornish chough and the legend of Lyonesse.&lt;SEP&gt;King Arthur is a legendary figure in British history and mythology, often associated with the ideals of chivalry, justice, and bravery. He is mentioned as a hero of the author's youth and is compared to Jack the Giantkiller. Key attributes include his legendary status, moral values, and cultural significance. He has a relationship with Jack the Giantkiller, both being seen as embodiments of justice and valor. Historically, he is a central figure in Cornish and British mythology. Notable actions include his leadership and the legend of Camelot.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 28}]</data>
</node>
<node id="LAND'S END">
  <data key="d0">LAND'S END</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A coastal area in England known for its natural beauty and dramatic rock formations. It is a popular destination for visitors seeking scenic views and recreational activities. The location is central to the narrative, serving as the setting for the day's events. It is associated with historical figures and legends, such as Tristram and Iseult. The Land's End is also mentioned in relation to other nearby places like Sennen and Penzance.&lt;SEP&gt;A geographical feature at the westernmost point of England, known for its dramatic coastal views. It is mentioned as a destination for the travelers, and they plan to visit it the next day. The place is described as being surrounded by natural beauty and historical significance.&lt;SEP&gt;A geographical location known for its dramatic cliffs and coastal views. It is situated at the westernmost point of the mainland of England, offering a panoramic view of the Atlantic Ocean. This place is significant for its natural beauty and historical associations, including stories of shipwrecks and local folklore. It is also mentioned as a place where one can experience solitude and reflection.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in Cornwall, England, known for its dramatic cliffs and coastal views. It is the destination of the narrator's journey and holds historical significance due to the story of the captain and his wife. It is central to the narrative and represents the culmination of the narrator's desire to visit it.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in Cornwall, England, known for its dramatic coastline and natural beauty. It is mentioned as the place where the sun drops into the sea, creating a golden mist. This location is significant for its scenic views and is central to the narrative of the sunset.&lt;SEP&gt;Land's End is a geographical location at the westernmost point of Cornwall, known for its dramatic cliffs and scenic beauty. It is a significant destination for the author, who has long dreamed of visiting it. Key attributes include its natural beauty, symbolic meaning, and personal significance to the author. It has a relationship with the author, who plans to visit it as part of their journey. Historically, it is a well-known landmark in Cornwall. Notable actions include the author's lifelong desire to see it.&lt;SEP&gt;Land's End is a prominent geographical location mentioned in the text. It is described as a place that the characters wished they could have stayed at, indicating its significance as a destination. The location is associated with the 'ghostly "might have been!"' which adds a sense of mystery and longing. It is also referenced as a point of interest that the characters wanted to see before their travels ended.&lt;SEP&gt;Land's End is the westernmost point of the mainland of England, located in Cornwall. It is described as a long peninsula stretching into the horizon where the sea meets the sky. It is associated with the legend of Lyonesse and the idea of Arthur's return.&lt;SEP&gt;The extreme western point of the island, described as a place with a narrow green plateau and a view of the sea. It is the final destination of the journey and is associated with the First and Last Inn, a local pub that serves food on Sundays.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca4427317d91b282aad105e4c1fcb34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fb3571cbea81efe6dfda2aa2199fdbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5e39286406a99236327034e4840f578&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4223cdc7e9877a27944679ddd0c14739&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 34}]</data>
</node>
<node id="1ST OF SEPTEMBER, 1881">
  <data key="d0">1ST OF SEPTEMBER, 1881</data>
  <data key="d1">DATE</data>
  <data key="d2">The 1st of September, 1881, is the date when the journey begins. It marks the start of the author's planned trip to Cornwall. Key attributes include its significance as the starting point of the journey. It has a relationship with the journey itself, serving as the beginning of the adventure. Historically, it is a specific moment in time that the author remembers. Notable actions include the departure from Exeter.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
</node>
