  <data key="d0">HYDRANGEAS</data>
  <data key="d1">PLANT</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrangeas are flowering plants mentioned in the text, noted for their large size in the region. They are described as growing abundantly in gardens, contributing to the beauty of the landscape. Hydrangeas are popular ornamental plants, often associated with gardens and floral displays.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
</node>
<node id="EUCALYPTUS">
  <data key="d0">EUCALYPTUS</data>
  <data key="d1">PLANT</data>
  <data key="d2">Eucalyptus is a type of tree mentioned in the text, described as growing into a forest in the region. It is known for its fast growth and adaptability, making it a significant species in the local ecosystem. Eucalyptus trees are often used for timber and have medicinal properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
</node>
<node id="OSMUNDA REGALIS">
  <data key="d0">OSMUNDA REGALIS</data>
  <data key="d1">PLANT</data>
  <data key="d2">Osmunda regalis is a type of fern mentioned in the text, found in the woods of Trelowarren. It is a rare and valuable plant, often sought after for its beauty and ecological significance. Osmunda regalis is a symbol of the natural diversity and richness of the Cornish landscape.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-743cd293533b6212903371567d44500f</data>
</node>
<node id="ST. RUMON">
  <data key="d0">ST. RUMON</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">St. Rumon is an Irish bishop who is said to have settled on Goonhilly Down, far from the world, and made a cell and oratory. His memory and name are preserved in the villages of Ruan Major and Ruan Minor. He represents a significant religious figure in the region's history.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3755e64526057a08de86199d3391703</data>
</node>
<node id="GOONHILLY DOWN">
  <data key="d0">GOONHILLY DOWN</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A geographical feature on the Cornish coast, described as a high plain with a sparkling sea beyond it. It is mentioned as a place where the characters wander and explore, highlighting its natural beauty and significance in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;Goonhilly Down is a wide, treeless waste in Cornwall that was once believed to be a forest full of wild beasts. It is now a flat, bare area known for its historical significance and unique geological features. The name comes from the old Cornish language, meaning 'hunting ground.'</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3755e64526057a08de86199d3391703&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cff43d4c0b87d4d234e1ded07f8597b</data>
</node>
<node id="CORNISH">
  <data key="d0">CORNISH</data>
  <data key="d1">LANGUAGE</data>
  <data key="d2">Cornish is a language spoken by Mary Mundy and is described as having a kindliest of Cornish hearts. It is part of the cultural identity of the region and is used in the dialogue of the characters.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cornish language is an extinct tongue that survives only in the names of places and people in Cornwall. It is referenced as part of the region's cultural heritage and historical identity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3755e64526057a08de86199d3391703&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
</node>
<node id="CORNISH HEATH">
  <data key="d0">CORNISH HEATH</data>
  <data key="d1">PLANT</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of flower that is described as being abundant in the garden and is often associated with the Cornish coast. It is mentioned alongside marigolds, carnations, and mignonette.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cornish heath, scientifically known as _erica vagans_, is a plant species found exclusively in certain areas of Cornwall and a district of Portugal. It is noted for its beauty and unique botanical characteristics, such as the position of its anthers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3755e64526057a08de86199d3391703&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12e1d0ddbd52f7c9b5db9fe78dbfee83</data>
</node>
<node id="SERPENTINE">
  <data key="d0">SERPENTINE</data>
  <data key="d1">MATERIAL</data>
  <data key="d2">Serpentine is a type of rock found along the coast of Cornwall, particularly near the Lizard. It is described as having a reddish and greenish color, and it is used to make ornaments, candlesticks, and brooches. Its presence is linked to the geological features of the region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3755e64526057a08de86199d3391703</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 22}]</data>
</node>
<node id="COVE">
  <data key="d0">COVE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific area on the Cornish coast that is described as picturesque and filled with natural beauty. It is a small cove with tiny pools, sea-weeds, and sea-anemones, which the characters discover during their adventure. This location is significant as it becomes a focal point of the story.</data>
