<node id="JOHN CURGENVEN">
  <data key="d0">JOHN CURGENVEN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A man who is part of the group exploring the Lizard area. He is described as knowledgeable about the Lizard Lights and their functionality. He provides information about the fog-horn and the steam-engine that powers the lights.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who is part of the local community and was recognized by the narrator as a friend. He greeted the narrator with a noble demeanor, reminiscent of a medieval knight. John Curgenven is an individual who appears to be well-respected among the villagers and is associated with the rectory tea event.&lt;SEP&gt;John Curgenven is a Cornish fisherman described as having a weather-beaten, sharp-lined face with a paternal smile and keen, kindly eyes. He is one of the oldest families in Cornwall and is known for his kindness to young people and the 'old lady.' His name is spelled 'Cur-gen-ven,' and he is portrayed as a figure of quiet dignity and pride in his heritage.&lt;SEP&gt;John Curgenven is a character who appears in the narrative. He is described as being active and helpful, offering to guide the narrator and her companion. He brings fish as a gift and shows concern for their safety. His role is that of a local guide and a friendly individual who provides information about the area.&lt;SEP&gt;John Curgenven is a fisherman known for his bravery and dedication. He is described as having a 'stout arm' and a 'fearless, honest face.' He rescued people from the wreck of the Brest and was involved in the local community. His actions and personality make him a central figure in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;John Curgenven is a person mentioned in the narrative who is associated with boating activities. He is described as having a boat ready at Church Cove and being chivalrous and careful during the boat trip. His presence and availability are crucial to the group's ability to visit the sea-caves.&lt;SEP&gt;John Curgenven is a person mentioned in the text as a guide who was not available when the narrator sought a boat. He is described as someone who could navigate the stormy seas, indicating his expertise and experience. Although he is not directly involved in the narrative, his absence highlights the challenges faced by the narrator and their companions in exploring the area. His profession as a boatman is significant in the context of the story.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e1d2b2559cbc455b5cb2409b4efdaf8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e302b5e22b09ab16666a0e39faaadeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
