<node id="TENNYSON">
  <data key="d0">TENNYSON</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Tennyson is a poet known for his work on the Arthurian legends, particularly his poem "Idylls of the King." In this text, he is referenced as the author who created or preserved the image of King Arthur as an ideal knight. His description of King Arthur's eyes is used to compare with the appearance of John Curgenven.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}]</data>
</node>
<node id="LYONESSE">
  <data key="d0">LYONESSE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A legendary land that was said to have been swallowed by the sea. It is referenced in the story of the Trevelyans, where an ancestor of the family saved himself by swimming his horse to safety. This place is tied to the legend of the rock of Goldsithney and represents a mythical past.&lt;SEP&gt;Lyonesse is a mythical land said to be submerged beneath the sea, mentioned in the context of Cornish folklore. It is associated with the legend of King Arthur and is described as a fair land that might lie beyond the horizon, where the sun drops into the sea.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 35}]</data>
</node>
<node id="CORNISH CHOUGH">
  <data key="d0">CORNISH CHOUGH</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">Cornish chough is a bird species native to Cornwall, mentioned as a symbol of the legend of King Arthur. It is said to haunt the Cornish coast, representing the lingering presence of Arthur in local folklore.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 28}]</data>
</node>
<node id="KYNANCE">
  <data key="d0">KYNANCE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall known for its natural beauty and historical significance. It is described as a place where the narrator and others attended a tea event at the rectory. Kynance is also mentioned in the context of a cornfield and a garden filled with flowers.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in Cornwall mentioned as a destination for the group. It is referenced in the context of the path taken by the group during their journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Kynance Cove is a location mentioned in the narrative, described as a beautiful place with natural features such as waves, rocks, and caves. It is the destination for the ladies in the story and is associated with the beauty of nature and the experience of the characters. The location plays a central role in the narrative, serving as a setting for various activities and reflections.&lt;SEP&gt;Kynance is a location mentioned in the text, likely referring to a coastal area or cliffside. It is described as a place that may not be suitable for travel due to tides, indicating its geographical significance.&lt;SEP&gt;Kynance is a location referenced in the narrative, likely a coastal area near the Lizard. It is mentioned as a destination for the group and is part of the scenic and natural landscape described in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e302b5e22b09ab16666a0e39faaadeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ee024234b415f45ea0006087720bb09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a99969e1d14b8295d2ca8754d35856</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}]</data>
</node>
<node id="COAST-GUARD">
  <data key="d0">COAST-GUARD</data>
  <data key="d1">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d2">An organization responsible for the safety of ships and seafarers along the coast. They are described as 'the pick of the service,' and their primary duty is to save lives, especially during times of shipwrecks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 30}]</data>
</node>
<node id="LIFE-BOAT HOUSE">
  <data key="d0">LIFE-BOAT HOUSE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A building housing the life-boat, which is used to rescue people during shipwrecks. It is mentioned as being located near the Lizard and having a slit in its door for contributions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 30}]</data>
</node>
<node id="KENNACK SANDS">
  <data key="d0">KENNACK SANDS</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall, England, known for its beautiful sandy beach and natural scenery. It is described as resembling the 'yellow sands' where Shakespeare's elves were bidden to 'take hands.'&lt;SEP&gt;Kennack Sands is a location mentioned in the text, likely a beach or coastal area. It is part of the planned route for the day, indicating its relevance to the journey.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12e1d0ddbd52f7c9b5db9fe78dbfee83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ee024234b415f45ea0006087720bb09</data>
</node>
<node id="CADGWITH">
  <data key="d0">CADGWITH</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall that is mentioned as a destination for the group. It is described as having a 'splendid cliff-walk' and being connected to Poltesco. The place is associated with the 'guide' and the 'young ladies,' and it represents a continuation of the journey through the coastal landscape.&lt;SEP&gt;A small fishing village located on the coast, known for its natural beauty and mild climate. It is described as one of the pleasantest nooks imaginable, sheltered from harsh weather by the surrounding land. The village is home to a small inn and has a close-knit community. Its location near the Devil's Frying-Pan makes it a notable place for visitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Cadgwith is a location mentioned in the text, likely a village or coastal town. It is part of the planned route for the day, showing its significance in the journey.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ee024234b415f45ea0006087720bb09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-436157d1f1611fd4639ef4c3e3e77cf8</data>
</node>
<node id="MULLION">
  <data key="d0">MULLION</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall where the travelers stopped. It is described as having an old church with historical significance. The church had ancient oak benches and was maintained by a vicar with archaeological interests. The place is associated with local traditions and stories, including one about Reverend Thomas Flavel and his ghost.&lt;SEP&gt;A place in Cornwall, mentioned as a stop on the route to Marazion. It is associated with a hotel and a church, suggesting it is a small, possibly historic settlement.&lt;SEP&gt;Mullion is a location mentioned in the text, likely a coastal area or village. It is part of the planned route for the day, indicating its relevance to the journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Mullion is a village mentioned in the text. It is a location where the travelers stay and is connected to the Old Inn. The village is described as having a distinct climate compared to other areas nearby, and it is near the Lizard and Mullion Cove.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59fd7b8af627bbbd404acb5ac88aef98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ee024234b415f45ea0006087720bb09</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
