<node id="DEVIL'S FRYING-PAN">
  <data key="d0">DEVIL'S FRYING-PAN</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A natural amphitheatre located near Cadgwith, known for its dramatic landscape and unique geological features. It is described as a two-acre area enclosed by a semi-circular slope covered with grass and flowers. The site is a major attraction in the area, with a serpentine arch and colorful rocks surrounding it. It is considered a dangerous place due to the strong waves and jagged rocks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
</node>
<node id="PARSON">
  <data key="d0">PARSON</data>
  <data key="d1">PROFESSION</data>
  <data key="d2">A religious figure who resides in the area and is described as kind and caring towards the invalids in the village. The parson and his wife are noted for their hospitality and willingness to look after those in need, whether rich or poor. Their role in the community highlights the social fabric of the village.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}]</data>
</node>
<node id="FISHER-WOMEN">
  <data key="d0">FISHER-WOMEN</data>
  <data key="d1">PROFESSION</data>
  <data key="d2">Women involved in the fishing industry in the village. They are described as being curious about the narrator and watching them closely as they climbed the cliff. Their presence reflects the local economy and the daily life of the villagers, who rely heavily on the sea for sustenance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 20}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 42}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 44}]</data>
</node>
<node id="INVALIDS">
  <data key="d0">INVALIDS</data>
  <data key="d1">DISEASE</data>
  <data key="d2">Individuals with health conditions who choose to reside in Cadgwith due to its mild climate. The village is noted for having an inn that is comfortable despite its simple exterior, and the parson and his wife are said to take care of these individuals. This reflects the village's reputation as a place of rest and recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}]</data>
</node>
<node id="FISHERMAN">
  <data key="d0">FISHERMAN</data>
  <data key="d1">PROFESSION</data>
  <data key="d2">A man engaged in the fishing industry. He is described as standing with his hands in his pockets, observing the narrator as they climbed the cliff. His presence and actions reflect the local way of life and the reliance on the sea for livelihood.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
</node>
<node id="RAGGED GIRL">
  <data key="d0">RAGGED GIRL</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A young girl from the village who is described as unkempt and tongue-tied. She is responsible for providing milk to the narrator and is portrayed as unfamiliar with modern currency, treating the penny as a rare curiosity. Her character highlights the simplicity and traditional lifestyle of the villagers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
</node>
<node id="MARY MUNDY">
  <data key="d0">MARY MUNDY</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A woman named Mary Mundy who ran a cottage with her brother. She greeted the travelers and expressed disappointment when they did not intend to stay. She showed interest in the professor, indicating a connection or familiarity with him. Her interaction with the travelers highlights her friendly and welcoming nature.&lt;SEP&gt;Mary Mundy is a central character in the narrative, known for her hospitality and role in providing tea to the travelers. She is described as a bright, brown-faced woman with red cheeks and black eyes, and is respected by the community. Her actions are significant in creating a welcoming environment for the visitors. She is also connected to the Professor, who is mentioned as someone whose friends she welcomes. Mary Mundy's presence brings comfort and warmth to the setting.&lt;SEP&gt;Mary Mundy is a central figure in the narrative, admired by the Professor for her beauty and presence. She plays a significant role in the group's journey and is associated with the setting of Cornish land. Her name is mentioned in a poetic line that highlights her importance. She is also connected to the broader social network at the Lizard.&lt;SEP&gt;Mary Mundy is a central figure in the narrative, known for her hospitality at the Old Inn. She is described as being essential to the operation of the inn, and her absence causes concern. Her presence is highly valued by the locals and visitors alike. She is also connected to the 'gentleman' through a poem and a portrait.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}]</data>
</node>
<node id="MARY'S BROTHER">
  <data key="d0">MARY'S BROTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Mary's brother is a character who appears when Mary is away. He is described as being somewhat helpless without her and is responsible for managing the inn during her absence. He is portrayed as friendly and willing to go out of his way to help the travelers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}]</data>
</node>
<node id="MULLION COVE">
  <data key="d0">MULLION COVE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Mullion Cove is a coastal area described as being a mile away from Mullion village. It is noted for its sea-caves and rocky terrain. The cove is accessible only at certain times and is used as a fish cellar. It is a significant location for the travelers' journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Mullion Cove is a coastal area known for its sea-caves and natural beauty. It serves as the primary setting for the narrative, where the characters explore the caves and experience the environment. The cove is described as being enclosed by large rocks and accessible only at low tide. Its significance lies in its role as a place of adventure and discovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad3c1e9f9f896847e83e5929f169df65</data>
</node>
<node id="PRADENACK DOWN">
  <data key="d0">PRADENACK DOWN</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall with an old church. The church is described as small and old, with a detached belfry. It has a history of shipwrecks and tales of lost treasures, including a mysterious baby found on the shore. The place is steeped in local folklore and historical significance.&lt;SEP&gt;Pradenack Down is a location mentioned in the text. It is part of the landscape that the travelers traverse while moving from the Lizard to Mullion. It is described as a bleak down and is part of the natural terrain in the region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1</data>
</node>
<node id="HORSE">
  <data key="d0">HORSE</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">The horse is an animal that plays a key role in the narrative. It is described as lively and used for transportation. Charles is responsible for the horse, and it is associated with the travelers' journey to Mullion Cove.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 19}]</data>
</node>
<node id="TEA">
  <data key="d0">TEA</data>
  <data key="d1">EVENT</data>
  <data key="d2">Tea is a key element in the narrative, served by Mary Mundy to the travelers. It is described as a welcome drink for weary travelers, hot, strong, and harmless. Tea plays a central role in the hospitality and comfort provided by Mary Mundy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tea is an event or activity that the travelers look forward to. It is mentioned as something that Mary Mundy would prepare for them. The travelers are assured that they will receive tea despite the challenges of the situation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
