</node>
<node id="LANDEWEDNACK">
  <data key="d0">LANDEWEDNACK</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall, England, known for its picturesque church and historical significance. It is the parish to which Lizard Town belongs. The church is notable for its architectural features and historical context, including being the most southerly church in England. It also has a rich history related to the Cornish language and the local community.&lt;SEP&gt;Landewednack is a location associated with the harvest festival. It is mentioned as the site of the event, where the characters plan to attend. The location is significant as it represents a cultural and social gathering point for the community.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a5dd17ac6c7028198a21adf7ff042f8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9baf49d70e5cdf906cbc1334a05b60e0</data>
</node>
<node id="LANDEWEDNACK CHURCH">
  <data key="d0">LANDEWEDNACK CHURCH</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A church located in Landewednack, known for its Norman doorway, hagioscope, and well-preserved architecture. It is the most southerly church in England and holds historical significance, including being the site where the last sermon in Cornish was preached in 1678. The churchyard contains graves of seafaring men and victims of the plague.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a5dd17ac6c7028198a21adf7ff042f8e</data>
</node>
<node id="SEA-BIRD">
  <data key="d0">SEA-BIRD</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">A dark sea-bird mentioned in the text, floating lazily between the sea and the sky. It is described as a 'dark speck on the perpetual blue,' indicating its presence in the natural environment. The bird is part of the scenery and contributes to the atmosphere of the scene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a5dd17ac6c7028198a21adf7ff042f8e</data>
</node>
<node id="BLACK DOG">
  <data key="d0">BLACK DOG</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">A large black dog that enters the church and sits in a pew behind the narrator. The dog behaves in an exemplary manner during the service, similar to Highland shepherds' dogs. It adds a unique element to the congregation and is noted for its decorum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a5dd17ac6c7028198a21adf7ff042f8e</data>
</node>
<node id="LAST SERMON IN CORNISH">
  <data key="d0">LAST SERMON IN CORNISH</data>
  <data key="d1">EVENT</data>
  <data key="d2">A historical event mentioned in the text, where the last sermon in Cornish was preached in 1678 at Landewednack Church. This event marks the end of the use of the Cornish language in religious contexts and signifies a cultural shift in the region.</data>
