  <data key="d0">LIFE-BOAT</data>
  <data key="d1">VEHICLE</data>
  <data key="d2">The life-boat is a vessel used for rescuing people from the sea. It is mentioned in the context of the Lizard Lights' operations, where it is deployed during emergencies. The life-boat plays a critical role in saving lives and is a symbol of the dedication of the coastguard men.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5d3bdb332242dccc51989976c4f0ce2e</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 31}]</data>
</node>
<node id="SUNSHINY CLIFF">
  <data key="d0">SUNSHINY CLIFF</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">The sunshiny cliff is a location where the narrator reflects on the events and stories shared by the young man. It provides a scenic backdrop for the narrative and is a place of contemplation and observation. The cliff is significant as it connects the natural environment with the human activities described.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5d3bdb332242dccc51989976c4f0ce2e</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 31}]</data>
</node>
<node id="MARAZION">
  <data key="d0">MARAZION</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A small town in Cornwall where the group stayed during their journey. Despite its picturesque name, it is described as mundane and unimpressive. The town is connected to the nearby St. Michael's Mount and is mentioned in the context of the group's stay and exploration.&lt;SEP&gt;A town in Cornwall, England, where the royal party is expected to arrive. It is described as having a small station and being the site of a crowd gathering to welcome the princess. The town is also mentioned as having a landlady who helps with arrangements for the travelers.&lt;SEP&gt;A town in Cornwall, noted for its historical significance and unique name. It was inhabited by Jewish colonists and has a connection to ancient tin trade. It is a destination for the group's next stop.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59fd7b8af627bbbd404acb5ac88aef98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 1}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 17}]</data>
</node>
<node id="GUNWALLOE">
  <data key="d0">GUNWALLOE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A place in Cornwall, referenced as a stop on the journey. It is associated with a church built by someone who was shipwrecked, indicating a historical or religious significance.&lt;SEP&gt;A small village in Cornwall where the travelers passed through. It is described as having healthy-looking children with big eyes and brown faces. This location reflects the rural and traditional lifestyle of the region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59fd7b8af627bbbd404acb5ac88aef98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="ESTHER">
  <data key="d0">ESTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Esther is a character who works as a handmaid at the Lizard. She is described as kind and helpful, and she bids the group a farewell. Her role is supportive, and she is remembered by the group for her kindness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19</data>
</node>
<node id="PENZANCE">
  <data key="d0">PENZANCE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall known for its scenic beauty and historical significance. It is described as having shrubs, flowers, and forest trees, and it is associated with King Arthur's legend. The area is also noted for its views and natural landscapes.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in Cornwall, known for its church-bells ringing and morning services. It is described as a place where people gather in their Sunday best, indicating its religious and community significance. The area is also noted for its natural beauty and peaceful atmosphere.&lt;SEP&gt;A town in England, noted for being 'genteel' and 'extremely civilised.' It is contrasted with the more rustic and charming Marazion, where the narrator stayed. Penzance is described as a place with beautiful surroundings but lacking the warmth and familiarity of the inn at Marazion. Its role in the narrative is to highlight the differences between urban sophistication and rural charm.&lt;SEP&gt;A town located near the Land's End, known for its harbor and tourist attractions. It is referenced in the context of the fisher wife and the local school, indicating its role in the daily life of the community. The town is also mentioned in relation to the Land's End and the surrounding areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Penzance is a destination mentioned in the text. The group plans to travel to Penzance via Helstone. It is a location that represents the end of their journey and is part of their planned route.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca4427317d91b282aad105e4c1fcb34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-744360fec46b02217eb1d4d9f455cb8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5e39286406a99236327034e4840f578</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 37}]</data>
</node>
<node id="CHURCH COVE">
  <data key="d0">CHURCH COVE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Church Cove is a location where John Curgenven has a boat ready. It is the site of the boating excursion that the group eventually participates in, and it is crucial to their ability to visit the sea-caves.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19</data>
</node>
<node id="10 A.M.">
  <data key="d0">10 A.M.</data>
  <data key="d1">TIME</data>
  <data key="d2">10 A.M. is a time mentioned in the text when the group plans to start their journey. It is later adjusted to 9 A.M. to accommodate the boating excursion. This time is important for the group's schedule and the timing of their activities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19</data>
</node>
<node id="MIDNIGHT">
  <data key="d0">MIDNIGHT</data>
  <data key="d1">TIME</data>
  <data key="d2">Midnight is a time mentioned in the text when the narrator goes out into the night. It is a moment of reflection and contemplation, highlighting the quiet and serene atmosphere of the setting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19</data>
</node>
<node id="SEA-CAVES">
  <data key="d0">SEA-CAVES</data>
  <data key="d1">NATURAL_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">The sea-caves are a natural attraction that the group had planned to visit. They are described as wonderful and something they had set their hearts upon. Their inability to visit them initially leads to a change in plans, and they eventually manage to see them through an adjustment in the schedule.&lt;SEP&gt;The sea-caves are natural formations along the coast of Cornwall, described as some of the finest things found in the region. They are mysterious and dangerous, with a dark interior and strong waves. The caves are significant for their beauty and the sense of adventure they provide. They are also linked to the legend of King Arthur and the natural environment of the area. The caves represent a unique and memorable experience for the narrator.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 19}]</data>
</node>
<node id="BREST">
  <data key="d0">BREST</data>
  <data key="d1">VEHICLE</data>
  <data key="d2">The Brest is an emigrant ship that sank near the Lizard coast. It is mentioned as a significant event in the narrative, with John describing how he helped rescue passengers. The wreck is associated with a tragic night when many lives were lost, and it serves as a focal point of the story.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
</node>
<node id="LIZARD COAST">
  <data key="d0">LIZARD COAST</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">The Lizard coast is a location along the English coast where the events of the narrative take place. It is described as 'magnificent' and 'awful,' highlighting its natural beauty and danger. The coast is associated with the shipwreck and the caves, making it a key setting in the story.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}]</data>
</node>
<node id="RAVEN'S UGO">
  <data key="d0">RAVEN'S UGO</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Raven's Ugo is a cave accessible by boat, located near the Lizard coast. It is described as having a pair of ravens nesting at the entrance and being used for smuggling in the past. Its historical significance and natural features make it an important location in the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
</node>
<node id="DOLOR UGO">
  <data key="d0">DOLOR UGO</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Dolor Ugo is a cave that is now inaccessible due to its narrow entrance and deep water. It is described as a 'solemn place' with a clear, green floor and colorful walls. Its mysterious nature and historical use for smuggling add to its significance in the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
</node>
<node id="WRECK OF THE BREST">
  <data key="d0">WRECK OF THE BREST</data>
  <data key="d1">EVENT</data>
  <data key="d2">The shipwreck of the Brest is a major event in the narrative. It occurred on a winter night, and John helped rescue passengers from the wreck. The event is described as 'awful' and is remembered by John through a physical injury, emphasizing its impact on the characters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}]</data>
</node>
<node id="SMUGGLING">
  <data key="d0">SMUGGLING</data>
  <data key="d1">CRIME</data>
  <data key="d2">Smuggling is a historical activity associated with the caves, particularly Raven's Ugo. It is mentioned as a past practice where kegs of spirits were hidden in the caves. This adds to the caves' historical and cultural significance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
</node>
<node id="THE MISTRESS">
  <data key="d0">THE MISTRESS</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A woman who offered to dry the travelers' clothes. She is described as hardworking, decent, and meticulous about cleanliness. Her house was noted for being spotless, and she seemed proud of her tidiness. She also mentioned that her home wasn't overly tidy that day due to washing, but she emphasized that it was normal in the area. Her actions reflect a strong sense of hospitality and pride in her work.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="HER BROTHER">
  <data key="d0">HER BROTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A man who lived with Mary Mundy and greeted the travelers. He was part of the family running the cottage and shared in the conversation with the travelers. His presence suggests a close relationship with Mary and possibly a shared responsibility in managing their home.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1</data>
</node>
<node id="REVEREND THOMAS FLAVEL">
  <data key="d0">REVEREND THOMAS FLAVEL</data>
  <data key="d1">TITLE</data>
  <data key="d2">A person with the title of Reverend, known for his work in the church and for being a 'ghost-layer.' He died in 1682, and his monument includes an epitaph reflecting the religious sentiments of the time. His legacy is preserved in local tradition, particularly regarding his ghost being laid by another clergyman.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1</data>
</node>
<node id="YOUNG WOMAN">
  <data key="d0">YOUNG WOMAN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A young woman who is described as 'comely' and has a baby in her arms and another at her gown. She is kind and generous, allowing the travelers to take apples from her tree. Her role is significant as she provides food and hospitality to the travelers. She is associated with the Cornish culture and is part of the local community. She also shares a personal story about her past with the narrator.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 37}]</data>
</node>
<node id="HONEST CORNISHMAN">
  <data key="d0">HONEST CORNISHMAN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A middle-aged man who is described as tall, gaunt, and with a shrewd, kindly face. He is a mechanical engineer and has a strong sense of loyalty and devotion to the family he once worked for. His role is significant as he is the host of the travelers and shares his personal history with them. He is associated with Cornish culture and is known in the town of Helstone.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}]</data>
</node>
<node id="WIFE">
  <data key="d0">WIFE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A middle-aged, comely matron who is the wife of the honest Cornishman. She is described as being in an afternoon gown and cap, and she insists on making tea for the travelers. Her role is significant as she provides hospitality and is part of the Cornish community. She is associated with the family and is known for her kindness.&lt;SEP&gt;A woman who survived a shipwreck but later died after falling into the water. Her story is recounted by the sailor and serves as a poignant example of the risks of the sea. Her death has a profound emotional impact on the captain and the narrator.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 36}]</data>
</node>
<node id="LIGHTHOUSE">
  <data key="d0">LIGHTHOUSE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A lighthouse that was built by the honest Cornishman. It is described as a significant achievement for him, and he is proud of his work. The lighthouse is associated with the Cornish coast and is a symbol of his professional expertise and dedication.&lt;SEP&gt;A structure built by the man and his uncle, which was lit by the Emperor. It is a significant landmark in the narrative, representing the collaboration between the man and his family. The lighthouse is tied to a historical event and is a symbol of pride and achievement. Its construction and the Emperor's involvement highlight the importance of this location in the story.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}]</data>
</node>
<node id="APPLE TREE">
  <data key="d0">APPLE TREE</data>
  <data key="d1">PLANT</data>
  <data key="d2">A fruit tree that the travelers attack for apples. The tree is described as being hung thickly with delicious fruit. The apple tree is significant as it provides food for the travelers and is part of the Cornish landscape. It is associated with the local community and the natural abundance of the region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 37}]</data>
</node>
<node id="BABY">
  <data key="d0">BABY</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">A baby described as 'a little dead baby in its cap and night-gown, with a necklace of coral beads.' This baby is mentioned in the context of a scene that the travelers encounter during their journey. The baby is significant as it represents the innocence and vulnerability of life, and it is associated with the Cornish culture and the natural environment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
</node>
<node id="THE MAN">
  <data key="d0">THE MAN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A middle-aged, comely matron who is described as being very hospitable and dedicated to her family. She is portrayed as someone who takes pride in her role as a hostess and is deeply connected to the traditions of her community. Her actions reflect a strong sense of duty and affection towards her husband and guests. She is also associated with the preparation of tea, which is seen as an important social ritual.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}]</data>
</node>
<node id="HIS WIFE">
  <data key="d0">HIS WIFE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A middle-aged, comely matron who is described as being very hospitable and dedicated to her family. She is portrayed as someone who takes pride in her role as a hostess and is deeply connected to the traditions of her community. Her actions reflect a strong sense of duty and affection towards her husband and guests. She is also associated with the preparation of tea, which is seen as an important social ritual.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}]</data>
</node>
<node id="EMPEROR">
  <data key="d0">EMPEROR</data>
  <data key="d1">TITLE</data>
  <data key="d2">A figure of authority and respect, who is mentioned as having lit the lighthouse. His involvement in the event adds a layer of historical and symbolic significance to the lighthouse. The Emperor is not directly involved in the narrative but is referenced as a key player in the event that shaped the man's life and legacy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}]</data>
</node>
<node id="WAX CANDLE">
  <data key="d0">WAX CANDLE</data>
  <data key="d1">OBJECT</data>
  <data key="d2">A small wax candle that was used by the Emperor to light the lighthouse. It is preserved by the man as a cherished relic, symbolizing the historical event and the bond between the man and the Emperor. The candle is also associated with annual rituals, where the man honors the Emperor and the family. It represents memory, tradition, and the enduring nature of relationships.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}]</data>
</node>
<node id="LOE POOL">
  <data key="d0">LOE POOL</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A small lake in Cornwall, separated from the sea by a sandbar. It is mentioned as a place of natural beauty and historical interest. The Loe Pool is associated with local legends, including the work of the Cornwall man-demon, Tregeagle. It is also linked to the Mayor of Helstone, who periodically cuts through the bar for a ceremonial event.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
</node>
<node id="NONSLOE HOUSE">
  <data key="d0">NONSLOE HOUSE</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A house in Cornwall that is associated with the right to provide a boat and nets for the Prince of Wales or the Duke of Cornwall when they wish to fish in the Loe Pool. This house is mentioned as a curious example of historical tenure and local customs. It reflects the unique traditions and responsibilities of the area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
</node>
<node id="NORWEGIAN BOATMAN">
  <data key="d0">NORWEGIAN BOATMAN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A Norwegian man who was shipwrecked and settled in the area. He is described as having a feeble command of English but an honest face. He serves as a boatman for the group, taking them out to sea for bathing and exploring St. Michael's Mount.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 1}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="GOLDSITHNEY">
  <data key="d0">GOLDSITHNEY</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A historical site in Cornwall, known for its association with the Trevelyans. It is mentioned in the context of a legend where an ancestor of the family escaped the sinking of Lyonesse by swimming his horse to safety. The rock is a symbol of the region's rich folklore.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 35}]</data>
</node>
<node id="CORMORAN">
  <data key="d0">CORMORAN</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A historical figure from Cornish legend, known as the giant Cormoran. He is said to have been killed by the valiant Cornishman Jack. His story is part of the lore surrounding St. Michael's Mount and contributes to the mystical atmosphere of the location.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 1}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="ST. KEYNE">
  <data key="d0">ST. KEYNE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A saint whose legend is tied to St. Michael's Mount. She is described as a king's daughter who came on pilgrimage to the site. Her story adds to the spiritual and historical significance of the Mount.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 1}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="HENRY DE LA POMEROY">
  <data key="d0">HENRY DE LA POMEROY</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A historical figure who was taken prisoner and died in the Castle at St. Michael's Mount. His story is part of the Mount's long history and is mentioned in the context of the legends surrounding the site.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 1}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="SIR JOHN ARUNDEL">
  <data key="d0">SIR JOHN ARUNDEL</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A historical figure who was slain on the sands and buried in the Chapel at St. Michael's Mount. His death is part of the Mount's legendary history and is mentioned in the narrative as one of the many stories associated with the site.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 1}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="PERKIN WARBECK'S WIFE">
  <data key="d0">PERKIN WARBECK'S WIFE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">The wife of Perkin Warbeck, who sought refuge at St. Michael's shrine but was dragged from it. Her story is part of the Mount's legendary past and highlights the site's historical and religious significance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 1}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="ST. AUBYN">
  <data key="d0">ST. AUBYN</data>
  <data key="d1">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A noble family that bought St. Michael's Mount in 1660 and has inhabited it ever since. They are noted for receiving royal visitors, which contrasts with the legendary figures associated with the Mount. Their presence adds a layer of historical continuity to the site.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 1}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="ST. AUBYNS">
  <data key="d0">ST. AUBYNS</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who is part of the St. Aubyn family, known for living on St. Michael's Mount. They are described as having a long history of residing there and managing their household despite the challenges of the location. The St. Aubyns are associated with the historical and cultural significance of the Mount, and they are mentioned in connection with a daughter who fell from the battlements. Their role in the narrative is to represent the legacy and lifestyle of those who live on the Mount.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-744360fec46b02217eb1d4d9f455cb8a</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 1}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 18}]</data>
</node>
<node id="YOUNG GUIDE">
  <data key="d0">YOUNG GUIDE</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who guided the narrator through St. Michael's Mount. She is described as a member of the St. Aubyn family, possessing simple grace and sweet courtesy. Her role is to showcase the hospitality and cultural heritage of the Mount, and she is portrayed as a worthy representative of the historical figures like the fair ladies of King Arthur's knights. She contributes to the narrative by providing insight into the site's history and significance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-744360fec46b02217eb1d4d9f455cb8a</data>
