<node id="WHITESAND BAY">
  <data key="d0">WHITESAND BAY</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall, England, known for its natural beauty and historical significance. It is mentioned as the destination of the royal party and is associated with the St. Aubyns. The bay is described as being alive with boats during high water, indicating its importance as a coastal area.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in Cornwall, where the narrator witnessed an event involving a woman rescued from the sea. It is significant because it is where the sailor recalls a tragic incident involving the captain and his wife. The bay is part of the broader landscape of Land's End.&lt;SEP&gt;Whitesand Bay is a coastal area that the characters decided to visit for a final drive. It is described as a place with a wide sweep of sunshiny sand, making it an ideal spot for relaxation. The location is significant because it was chosen over other destinations like Botallack Mine, showing its appeal as a place for leisure and enjoyment.</data>
