<node id="TINTAGEL">
  <data key="d0">TINTAGEL</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall, England, known for its historical and legendary significance. It is the birthplace of King Arthur and is described as part of the legendary region of Cornwall. The place is visited by the travelers as part of their journey.&lt;SEP&gt;A location in the UK where the narrative takes place. It is described as a ruin that once was a fortress and is now a site of historical interest. The location is associated with the Arthurian legends and is a key setting for the reflection on history and chivalry.&lt;SEP&gt;Tintagel is a coastal village in Cornwall, England, known for its historic castle and association with the legend of King Arthur. It is a prominent location in the text, where the narrator mentions the wild sail and the dark, iron-bound coast. Tintagel is a place of natural beauty and historical significance, often linked to Arthurian lore.&lt;SEP&gt;Tintagel is a historic site located on the coast, known for its ruins and association with legends of King Arthur. It is described as a natural-artificial fortress and a landmark between legend and actual history. The site has been inhabited by various groups over centuries and is now a place of mystery and imagination. It is also linked to the story of Merlin and the 'little naked child' who grew up to be the 'stainless king.'&lt;SEP&gt;Tintagel is a location in Cornwall, England, known for its historical and mythological associations, particularly with King Arthur. It is described as a place of solitude and silence, with a church dating from Saxon times. The location is significant due to its connection with the Arthurian legend and its natural beauty. It is also a site of interest for visitors, with nearby caves and cliffs that hold both natural and cultural importance.&lt;SEP&gt;Tintagel is an ancient castle in Cornwall, referenced as the site where King Arthur was born. It is a significant location in Arthurian legend, symbolizing the beginning of Arthur's story. Tintagel is also mentioned as a place of historical and mythological importance, often linked to the early life of King Arthur.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 28}]</data>
</node>
<node id="CAMELFORD">
  <data key="d0">CAMELFORD</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">A location in Cornwall, England, which is the site of a castle and an ancient town. It is mentioned as one of the places the travelers pass through during their journey. The town is historically significant and is associated with Joseph Addison, M.P.&lt;SEP&gt;Camelford is a town in Cornwall, mentioned as the site of King Arthur's final battle. It is contrasted with the mythical Camelot, highlighting the difference between historical reality and legend. Camelford serves as a real-world location that connects to the Arthurian legend through its association with Arthur's death.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb</data>
