<node id="SHIP">
  <data key="d0">SHIP</data>
  <data key="d1">VEHICLE</data>
  <data key="d2">The ship is a vessel that carried a peal of bells to the cove. It was involved in a dramatic event where a storm arose after the pilot made a comment about the safe voyage. The ship sank, and only the pilot survived. This event is central to the narrative and highlights the dangers of the sea and the unpredictability of fate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}]</data>
</node>
<node id="PILOT">
  <data key="d0">PILOT</data>
  <data key="d1">PROFESSION</data>
  <data key="d2">The pilot is a person who guided the ship and was the only survivor of the shipwreck. He made a comment to the captain about the safe voyage, which was met with a response that led to the storm. His survival and the events surrounding him are significant to the story and reflect themes of fate and divine intervention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}]</data>
</node>
<node id="CHOUGH">
  <data key="d0">CHOUGH</data>
  <data key="d1">ANIMAL</data>
  <data key="d2">Choughs are a type of bird, specifically a species of crow, known for their distinctive red beaks and feet. They are mentioned as being almost extinct and are considered a notable bird in the text. Their presence in the village is described as a sign of peace and reverence, as Cornishmen avoid shooting them due to superstition. Choughs are also linked to the legend of King Arthur, as their shape is believed to carry his soul. They are an important part of the local ecosystem and cultural beliefs.&lt;SEP&gt;The chough is a bird species mentioned in the text, believed to be the shape of King Arthur's soul. It is associated with the ruins of Tintagel and is considered a symbol of the past. The text notes that no Cornishman likes to shoot a chough or a raven, highlighting its cultural and superstitious significance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
  <data key="d4">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 28}]</data>
</node>
<node id="PORTH HERN">
  <data key="d0">PORTH HERN</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Porth Hern is a location in Cornwall, described as a rocky hollow where a stream dances down and leaps into the sea. It is associated with the legend of King Arthur and is a place of natural beauty and mystery. The location is significant for its role in the narrative, as it is where the narrator and their companions seek a boat to explore the sea-caves. It is also connected to the broader landscape of Cornwall and its historical and cultural significance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
</node>
<node id="TINTAGEL CHURCH">
  <data key="d0">TINTAGEL CHURCH</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Tintagel Church is a historical building located on the edge of a cliff, offering a panoramic view of the sea. It dates back to Saxon times and is an important religious and cultural site. The church is significant for its architectural value and its association with the Arthurian legend. It is also a place of quiet reflection and solitude, as the narrator watches a sunset there. Its location and history make it a key element in the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
</node>
<node id="SLATE QUARRY">
  <data key="d0">SLATE QUARRY</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">The slate quarry is a location accessible only from the sea, where workers can be seen moving about like black emmets among the clefts of the rocks. It is a place of labor and danger, with workers using ropes to descend and work in challenging conditions. The quarry is significant for its role in the local economy and the physical demands of the work. It is also mentioned in the context of the narrator's journey and the dangers of the coastal environment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
</node>
<node id="LAUNCESTON">
  <data key="d0">LAUNCESTON</data>
  <data key="d1">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Launceston is a town in Cornwall, England, located near Tintagel. It is mentioned in the text as part of the journey between Tintagel and Launceston. While not as historically significant as Tintagel, Launceston is an important local settlement and serves as a reference point in the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62</data>
</node>
<node id="OLIVER CROMWELL">
  <data key="d0">OLIVER CROMWELL</data>
  <data key="d1">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d2">Oliver Cromwell was an English military and political leader who played a key role in the English Civil War and the establishment of the Commonwealth. The text draws a comparison between the varied records of King Arthur's grave and the three heads of Oliver Cromwell, highlighting the historical and symbolic nature of these figures.</data>
