<edge source="CORNWALL" target="AN UNSENTIMENTAL JOURNEY THROUGH CORNWALL">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The book 'An Unsentimental Journey Through Cornwall' is centered around the journey through the region of Cornwall. This relationship highlights the geographical focus of the book and the importance of Cornwall in the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-6196f7575ff952a3f5db2b2d296af03f</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CORNWALL" target="OLD HEN">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The 'old hen' has a direct relationship with Cornwall, as she plans to take the 'chickens' there on their journey. This relationship is driven by her desire to show the group the beauty and significance of the region. The impact of this relationship is that the 'old hen' is motivated to plan and execute the trip. Contextually, this relationship is rooted in her personal aspirations and the cultural significance of Cornwall. The relationship evolved as she developed a stronger connection to the region through her dreams and experiences. Notable actions include her decision to take the 'chickens' to Cornwall.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CORNWALL" target="COAST-GUARD">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Coast-guard and the people of Cornwall. The Coast-guard is described as being essential to the safety of the region, particularly during times of shipwrecks. Their work is vital to the community, and they are considered 'true Englishmen' who are fearless and faithful.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CORNWALL" target="COWS">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The cows have a direct relationship with the Cornish landscape, as their color is said to harmonize with the surrounding scenery. This relationship is significant because it emphasizes the connection between the natural environment and the local fauna. The cows are an integral part of the Cornish ecosystem and contribute to the visual and cultural identity of the region.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CORNWALL" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the travelers and the natural landscape of Cornwall is indirect but influential. The journey through the region exposes the travelers to various locations, each with its own historical and scenic significance. This relationship shapes their experience and contributes to the overall narrative of their journey.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CORNWALL" target="KING ARTHUR">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between King Arthur and Cornwall is based on the region's association with Arthurian legend. Cornwall is described as a place that could have produced a hero like King Arthur, given its wild and dramatic landscape. This connection highlights how the geography and culture of Cornwall are intertwined with the mythos of King Arthur.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CORNWALL" target="TINTAGEL">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between Tintagel and Cornwall is direct and geographical. Tintagel is a specific location within the broader region of Cornwall, and the text emphasizes the natural and historical features of both. This relationship reflects the way in which individual landmarks contribute to the cultural and historical identity of a larger area.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
