</edge>
<edge source="ST. MICHAEL'S MOUNT" target="NORWEGIAN BOATMAN">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The Norwegian boatman is connected to St. Michael's Mount through his role as a guide and boat operator. He takes the group to explore the Mount, providing access to the site and enhancing their experience of the location. His relationship with the Mount is both practical and symbolic, as he represents the local knowledge and tradition of the area.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. MICHAEL'S MOUNT" target="CORMORAN">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of the giant Cormoran is associated with St. Michael's Mount. He is said to have been killed by the valiant Cornishman Jack, and his story is part of the Mount's rich folklore. This relationship emphasizes the Mount's role as a site of legend and historical memory.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. MICHAEL'S MOUNT" target="ST. AUBYN">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The family of St. Aubyn has a long-standing connection to St. Michael's Mount, having purchased it in 1660 and continuing to inhabit it. Their presence adds a layer of historical continuity to the Mount, contrasting with the earlier legendary figures associated with the site. This relationship reflects the evolving history of the location.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. MICHAEL'S MOUNT" target="ST. KEYNE">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of St. Keyne is linked to St. Michael's Mount, as she is said to have come on pilgrimage to the site. Her story contributes to the Mount's spiritual and historical significance, reinforcing its role as a place of religious and cultural importance.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. MICHAEL'S MOUNT" target="HENRY DE LA POMEROY">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of Henry de la Pomeroy is connected to St. Michael's Mount, as he is said to have died in the Castle there. This relationship highlights the Mount's historical role as a site of both political and personal tragedy, adding depth to its legendary past.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. MICHAEL'S MOUNT" target="SIR JOHN ARUNDEL">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of Sir John Arundel is tied to St. Michael's Mount, as he is said to have been slain on the sands and buried in the Chapel. This relationship underscores the Mount's historical and tragic significance, linking it to the broader narrative of the region's past.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. MICHAEL'S MOUNT" target="PERKIN WARBECK'S WIFE">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of Perkin Warbeck's wife is associated with St. Michael's Mount, as she sought refuge there before being dragged from the shrine. This relationship illustrates the Mount's role as a site of both sanctuary and conflict, reflecting its complex historical and religious significance.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. MICHAEL'S MOUNT" target="ST. AUBYNS">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The St. Aubyns have a direct relationship with St. Michael's Mount, as they reside there and manage the estate. This relationship is significant because it reflects their long-standing connection to the location and their ability to maintain a household despite the challenges of the environment. The Mount is not just a physical place but also a cultural and historical landmark tied to the family's identity.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-744360fec46b02217eb1d4d9f455cb8a</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. MICHAEL'S MOUNT" target="YOUNG GUIDE">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The young guide has a direct relationship with St. Michael's Mount, as she works there and provides tours to visitors. This relationship is important because it highlights the role of local guides in preserving and sharing the history of the site. The guide's presence adds to the cultural richness of the Mount and helps visitors understand its significance.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-744360fec46b02217eb1d4d9f455cb8a</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. MICHAEL'S MOUNT" target="NARRATOR">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The narrator has a direct relationship with St. Michael's Mount, as they visited the location and experienced its unique features firsthand. This relationship is significant because it allows the narrator to provide a detailed account of the Mount's characteristics, including its historical value, natural beauty, and the challenges of living there. The visit contributes to the overall narrative and understanding of the location.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-744360fec46b02217eb1d4d9f455cb8a</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="FALMOUTH" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The travelers have a direct relationship with the location of Falmouth, as they plan to visit it and consider staying at the Green Bank Hotel. Their decision to avoid the new hotel and opt for the older one reflects their preference for quiet and traditional accommodations. This relationship highlights their travel preferences and expectations.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-4f15d171fef48d074f3c095117c010dc</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="FALMOUTH" target="NARRATOR">
  <data key="d5">1.2</data>
  <data key="d6">The narrator has a direct relationship with the location of Falmouth, as it is mentioned as a possible origin for the pic-nic parties that visit Kynance Sands. The location is associated with the movement of tourists and the broader context of the trip.&lt;SEP&gt;The relationship between the narrator and Falmouth is further shaped by the broader context of the trip and the interactions with other locations. The location's significance is tied to the overall journey and the experiences of the group.&lt;SEP&gt;The relationship between the narrator and Falmouth is indirect, as the location is referenced in the context of tourist activity and the movement of people. The location's influence is more subtle and contextual.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c527f463c6b54a8cb919a64d12423b24</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="OLD HEN" target="CHICKENS">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The 'old hen' has a direct relationship with the 'chickens,' as she is the one leading and guiding them on their journey. This relationship is characterized by authority and care, as the 'old hen' sets rules and ensures the safety and happiness of the group. The impact of this relationship is that the 'chickens' must follow her instructions to enjoy the journey. Contextually, this relationship reflects a mentorship dynamic. The relationship evolved as the 'old hen' took on the role of leader and planner. Notable actions include the 'old hen' setting rules and leading the group.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="OLD HEN" target="LAND'S END">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The 'old hen' has a second-order relationship with Land's End, as her journey to Cornwall includes a visit to this location. This relationship is indirect, as it stems from her broader goal of exploring Cornwall. The impact of this relationship is that it motivates her to plan the trip. Contextually, this relationship is tied to her lifelong dream of seeing Land's End. The relationship evolved as her desire to visit the location grew over time. Notable actions include her decision to make Land's End a destination on her journey.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="JACK THE GIANTKILLER" target="KING ARTHUR">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">Jack the Giantkiller and King Arthur have a direct relationship as both are depicted as symbols of justice, bravery, and moral strength. Their relationship is based on shared values and mythological significance. The impact of this relationship is that they both represent the ideals of the Cornish people. Contextually, this relationship highlights the cultural and historical legacy of Cornwall. The relationship evolved as the author came to appreciate both figures as sources of inspiration. Notable actions include their legendary deeds and the author's admiration for them.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="KING ARTHUR" target="JOHN CURGENVEN">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">John Curgenven is connected to the legend of King Arthur through his physical resemblance to the ideal knight described by Tennyson. This relationship suggests that the type of man represented by Arthur may still exist in modern Cornwall, as seen in John Curgenven's demeanor and appearance.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="KING ARTHUR" target="CORNISH CHOUGH">
  <data key="d5">0.5</data>
  <data key="d6">The Cornish chough is linked to the legend of King Arthur through local folklore. The text states that the chough is believed to haunt the Cornish coast, suggesting a symbolic connection between the bird and the mythical figure of Arthur.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="KING ARTHUR" target="LYONESSE">
  <data key="d5">0.4</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of Lyonesse is indirectly related to the belief in King Arthur's return. The text implies that the myth of Lyonesse, a lost land, is part of the same cultural tradition that keeps the legend of Arthur alive in the minds of the people of Cornwall.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="KING ARTHUR" target="TINTAGEL">
  <data key="d5">1.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between King Arthur and Tintagel is rooted in legend and historical significance. Tintagel is believed to be connected to King Arthur's story, particularly his birth and the location of his grave. This connection gives Tintagel a special place in Arthurian lore and makes it a site of interest for those exploring the myths surrounding King Arthur.&lt;SEP&gt;Tintagel had a second-order relationship with King Arthur, as it is associated with the legends of the 'stainless king' and the 'little naked child.' The site is believed to have been the landing-place of King Arthur and other Cornish heroes, linking it to the mythological and historical narratives of the region. This relationship reflects the cultural and historical significance of the location.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="KING ARTHUR" target="CHOUGH">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between King Arthur and the chough is symbolic, as the chough is believed to carry his soul. This connection is rooted in folklore and mythology, linking the bird to the legendary figure. The belief in this relationship reflects the cultural and spiritual significance of both entities. The chough's presence in the village is also influenced by this legend, as people avoid shooting them out of respect for the myth. This relationship highlights the deep-rooted traditions and beliefs of the local community.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="KING ARTHUR" target="OLIVER CROMWELL">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between King Arthur and Oliver Cromwell is a metaphorical one, drawn from the text's comparison of their respective legacies. Both figures are seen as historical icons, and the text uses the idea of multiple representations (like the three heads of Cromwell) to reflect on the various accounts of King Arthur's grave. This relationship underscores the theme of historical interpretation and memory.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9dca5f3a8de43d3e2bc3bc88b3703f62</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LAND'S END" target="LYONESSE">
  <data key="d5">0.5</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of Lyonesse is indirectly connected to the Cornish coast and the Land's End. The text suggests that the myth of Lyonesse, a submerged land, is part of the cultural and geographical imagination of the region, linking it to the natural landscape and the belief in Arthur's return.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LAND'S END" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the travelers and Land's End is indirect, as the destination influences their travel plans and decisions. Their anticipation of visiting Land's End shapes their overall journey and the choices they make along the way.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LAND'S END" target="NARRATOR">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the narrator and the Land's End is a direct experience of the journey's conclusion. The narrator reaches this location, which marks the end of the trip and is associated with the First and Last Inn, a key stop on the way.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-2ca4427317d91b282aad105e4c1fcb34</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LAND'S END" target="CHARACTERS">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the characters and Land's End is one of desire and longing. The characters express a wish to have stayed there, indicating that it holds a special significance for them. This relationship is direct and reflects the emotional connection the characters have with the location.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-1fb3571cbea81efe6dfda2aa2199fdbe</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LAND'S END" target="TRISTRAM AND ISEULT">
  <data key="d5">0.5</data>
  <data key="d6">The association of the Land's End with the legend of Tristram and Iseult forms a third-order relationship between the geographical location and the cultural narrative. This relationship underscores the way in which historical and mythological stories can influence the perception and interpretation of natural landmarks, adding layers of meaning to the physical environment.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-a5e39286406a99236327034e4840f578</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1ST OF SEPTEMBER, 1881" target="JOURNEY">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The 1st of September, 1881, has a direct relationship with the journey, as it marks the day the trip begins. This relationship is defined by the significance of the date in the narrative. The impact of this relationship is that it serves as the starting point of the adventure. Contextually, this relationship is tied to the author's personal history and the planning of the trip. The relationship evolved as the date became a key milestone in the journey. Notable actions include the departure from Exeter on this date.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-eaa5de23ba6f27901db4cc60fd6df0ae</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PLYMOUTH" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The travelers have a direct relationship with the location of Plymouth, as it is one of the first stops in their journey. They observe the parlour windows and other features of the city, indicating their interest in the area's architecture and culture. This relationship reflects their curiosity and engagement with the environment.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-4f15d171fef48d074f3c095117c010dc</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SALTASH" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The travelers have a direct relationship with the location of Saltash, as they pass through it and admire the bridge. The bridge is described as a remarkable structure, and the travelers' reaction to it shows their appreciation for the engineering and aesthetics of the area. This relationship highlights their admiration for the landscape.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-4f15d171fef48d074f3c095117c010dc</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GERRANS" target="OLD GENTLEMAN">
  <data key="d5">0.5</data>
  <data key="d6">The old gentleman has a second-order relationship with the location of Gerrans, as he mentions it as a quiet place to stay and offers advice about traveling there. Although the travelers do not ultimately go to Gerrans, the mention of the village influences their perception of the region and their travel choices.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-4f15d171fef48d074f3c095117c010dc</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="OLD GENTLEMAN" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The old gentleman has a direct relationship with the travelers, offering them advice about their journey in Cornwall. This relationship is based on their interaction in the carriage, where he shares his knowledge and opinions about the region. His advice influences the travelers' decisions, even if they choose not to follow it entirely.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-4f15d171fef48d074f3c095117c010dc</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="OLD GENTLEMAN" target="NARRATOR">
  <data key="d5">2.1</data>
  <data key="d6">The narrator and the old gentleman are connected through their shared recognition of the importance of caution and wisdom in the face of risk. This relationship is influenced by their common experiences and discussions about the behavior of young people.&lt;SEP&gt;The narrator and the old gentleman have a direct relationship based on their shared experience at Kynance Sands. This relationship evolves into a deeper connection as they discuss various topics and agree on their sentiments about youth and bravery.&lt;SEP&gt;The narrator and the old gentleman share a mutual understanding and agreement regarding the behavior of young people. They engage in a conversation that leads to a sense of camaraderie and shared values.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c527f463c6b54a8cb919a64d12423b24</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="FRIEND">
  <data key="d5">1.4</data>
  <data key="d6">The friend provided practical assistance to the narrator and her companions by helping them secure lodgings in the Lizard. This relationship is significant as it directly influenced the travelers' ability to continue their journey. The friend's actions had a positive impact on the travelers, ensuring they had a comfortable place to stay. The relationship evolved from a brief meeting in Falmouth to a supportive connection that aided the travelers' journey. The friend's advice led to the decision to move to the Lizard, which was a key event in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;The narrator's journey from Falmouth to the Lizard involved a second-order relationship through the influence of the friend's advice. This relationship is significant as it indirectly affected the travelers' movement from one location to another. The friend's recommendation had a lasting impact on the travelers' decisions, shaping their entire journey. The relationship evolved from a direct interaction in Falmouth to an indirect influence on their travel plans. The friend's advice ultimately led to the decision to move to the Lizard, which was a key event in the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-85b0c1bb6db9932af72e242802e01101</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NARRATOR" target="CHARLES">
  <data key="d5">4.05</data>
  <data key="d6">Charles provides assistance and guidance to the narrator, helping her navigate a dangerous path. This relationship is characterized by care, support, and a sense of duty. Charles's actions ensure the narrator's safety and comfort during the journey, demonstrating a strong bond of trust and responsibility.&lt;SEP&gt;The narrator and Charles developed a friendly and trusting relationship during their journey. This relationship is significant as Charles provided reliable transportation and became an important part of their travel experience. The relationship had a positive impact on the travelers, as Charles's professionalism and kindness made their journey more enjoyable. The relationship began with a simple transaction but deepened over time as Charles proved to be a trustworthy and helpful driver. Charles's reliability ensured that the travelers reached their destinations safely.&lt;SEP&gt;The narrator and Charles have a professional relationship, as Charles is responsible for transporting the narrator and others. Their interaction involves planning and executing travel, showing a level of trust and reliance.&lt;SEP&gt;The narrator relies on Charles for transportation and support during the journey. This relationship is built on trust and dependence, as the narrator is physically unable to navigate the terrain alone. Charles's presence and efforts significantly impact the narrator's ability to complete the journey safely.&lt;SEP&gt;The relationship between the narrator and Charles involves guidance and companionship during the journey. Charles advises the narrator to take a guide and helps navigate the trip, showing a direct connection between them as they travel together.</data>
