<edge source="JOHN CURGENVEN" target="TENNYSON">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">Tennyson's depiction of King Arthur influences the perception of John Curgenven's appearance. The text notes that the eyes of John Curgenven are compared to those of King Arthur as described by Tennyson, creating a direct link between the literary representation and the real-life individual.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="JOHN CURGENVEN" target="LIZARD LIGHTS">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between John Curgenven and the Lizard Lights. He provides detailed information about the lights, their visibility, and the machinery that powers them. This indicates his familiarity and expertise regarding the lighthouse system.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-2d3c8ddcaba7527f7fcbf3bec3621635</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="JOHN CURGENVEN" target="KYNANCE COVE">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">John Curgenven guides the narrator and her companion to Kynance Cove. This relationship is direct and involves his role as a local guide. His knowledge of the area helps them navigate safely.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-e1d2b2559cbc455b5cb2409b4efdaf8e</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="JOHN CURGENVEN" target="GROUP">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The group's initial plan to visit the sea-caves falls through due to the unavailability of John Curgenven's boat. This relationship highlights the importance of John Curgenven's availability and the impact of his absence on the group's plans. It leads to a change in the schedule and ultimately results in the successful visit to the caves.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="KYNANCE COVE" target="MOUNT'S BAY">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The natural landscape of Kynance Cove, Gull Rock, and Asparagus Island is connected to the broader geography of Mount's Bay and the Land's End. These locations form part of the coastal scenery that the group observes and reflects upon, contributing to the overall atmosphere of the narrative.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-df082bbea6d90a549cd8b56e6131733a</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LYONESSE" target="GOLDSITHNEY">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of the fair land of Lyonesse is connected to the rock of Goldsithney, as it is said that the land was engulfed by the sea and an ancestor of the Trevelyans saved himself by swimming his horse to safety. This relationship reinforces the rock's role as a symbol of the region's mythical past and its connection to the Trevelyans.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LYONESSE" target="TREVELYANS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of the fair land of Lyonesse is also tied to the Trevelyans, as an ancestor of the family is said to have saved himself by swimming his horse to safety when the land was swallowed by the sea. This relationship highlights the Trevelyans' historical and mythological connection to the region and its legendary past.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="KYNANCE" target="LADIES">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The ladies' interaction with Kynance Cove leads to their exploration of various locations, including the Bellows and Asparagus Island. This relationship is indirect and reflects the broader context of their journey. The evolution of this relationship is tied to their curiosity and desire to experience the natural wonders of the area.</data>
