  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SEA" target="ARTHUR">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of Arthur is further connected to the natural landscape of Cornwall, including the sea and the cliffs. This third-order relationship illustrates how the myths of Arthur are woven into the environment, creating a sense of continuity between the past and the present. The sea, in particular, is associated with the legend of Lyonesse and the submerged lands beneath it.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PARSON" target="INVALIDS">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">There is a direct relationship between the parson and the invalids in the village. The parson and his wife are described as taking pleasure in looking after the invalids, regardless of their financial status. This relationship highlights the community's support system and the role of the parson in providing care and assistance to those in need.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PARSON" target="VILLAGERS">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the parson and the villagers is a second-order one, as the parson's kindness and care for the invalids influence the overall social dynamics of the village. His actions contribute to the sense of community and mutual support among the residents, reinforcing the importance of the parson's role in maintaining harmony and well-being.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MARY MUNDY" target="OLD INN">
  <data key="d5">1.7000000000000002</data>
  <data key="d6">Mary Mundy has a direct relationship with the Old Inn, as she is the one who serves the travelers there. This relationship is significant because it highlights her role as a host and her ability to provide a welcoming environment. The Old Inn is an important location in the narrative, serving as a place of rest and hospitality.&lt;SEP&gt;Mary Mundy is directly linked to the Old Inn, where she is the hostess and essential to its operation. Her absence causes concern among the locals and the travelers. The relationship between Mary and the inn is vital, as she is the one who ensures the guests are comfortable and the inn runs smoothly.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MARY MUNDY" target="GENTLEMAN">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">Mary Mundy is indirectly connected to the 'gentleman' through a poem and a portrait. The 'gentleman' is a notable figure in the region, and his poem about Mary is kept in her visitors' book. This relationship highlights the cultural significance of the 'gentleman' and his influence on the local community.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-e785b114eb81de96943779a460bab162</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MARY MUNDY" target="TEA">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">Mary Mundy has a direct relationship with tea, as she serves it to the travelers. This relationship is significant because it highlights her role as a host and her ability to provide comfort and nourishment. The tea is a central element of the hospitality offered by Mary Mundy, and it plays a crucial role in the travelers' experience.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MARY MUNDY" target="PROFESSOR">
  <data key="d5">1.5</data>
  <data key="d6">Mary Mundy has a direct relationship with the Professor, as she is described as being connected to his friends. This relationship is significant because it shows her role in the social network of the Professor and her willingness to welcome his associates. The relationship reflects the mutual respect and familiarity between Mary Mundy and the Professor.&lt;SEP&gt;The relationship between Mary Mundy and the Professor is one of admiration and respect. The Professor's comment about her being 'the brightest thing on Cornish land' highlights her significance and the positive impression she makes on others. This relationship contributes to the overall tone of the narrative, emphasizing her beauty and influence.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MARY MUNDY" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">Mary Mundy has a direct relationship with the travelers, as she is the one who welcomes them and provides them with a meal. This relationship is significant because it highlights her role as a host and her ability to create a comfortable and welcoming environment. The travelers' experience is greatly influenced by her hospitality and kindness.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c13d92c83041a8623279efdde8af1707</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MARY MUNDY" target="THE TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">Mary Mundy and her brother welcomed the travelers at their cottage and expressed disappointment when they did not plan to stay. Their relationship with the travelers indicates a friendly and hospitable attitude. They engaged in conversation, showing an interest in the travelers' lives and even mentioned the professor, suggesting a possible connection to him. This relationship reflects the warmth and community spirit of the locals.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MARY'S BROTHER" target="GENTLEMAN">
  <data key="d5">0.5</data>
  <data key="d6">Mary's brother is indirectly related to the 'gentleman' through his association with Mary Mundy. Although he does not have a direct connection to the 'gentleman', his role in managing the inn during Mary's absence is influenced by the cultural reverence for the 'gentleman' and his work.</data>
