<edge source="CLERGYMAN" target="YOUNG PEOPLE">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The clergyman expresses concern for the safety of the young people, particularly those attempting to climb dangerous rocks. This relationship is based on a sense of responsibility and care, highlighting the clergyman's role as a guardian figure. The impact of this relationship is evident in the warnings he gives and the influence he has on the actions of the characters.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f0a99969e1d14b8295d2ca8754d35856</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CLERICAL FRIEND OF KYNANCE" target="LAUNCE-FISHING">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The clerical friend of Kynance is indirectly related to the practice of launce-fishing. He provides information about the activity, describing its methods and significance. This relationship highlights the transmission of local knowledge and traditions.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-0a11868bbedb00db5ad94f79da20acdd</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MARAZION" target="PRINCESS FREDERICA">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the royal party and the residents of Marazion is characterized by anticipation and curiosity. The townspeople gather to welcome the princess, showing their interest in the event. This relationship highlights the impact of the royal visit on the local community and the excitement it generates.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GUNWALLOE" target="THE TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The travelers passed through Gunwalloe, where they observed the old church and heard stories of shipwrecks and lost treasures. This relationship links the travelers to the local folklore and historical events of the area. The stories of the lost baby and other artifacts illustrate the deep connection between the people and their environment, adding a layer of mystery and richness to the journey.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PENZANCE" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the travelers and Penzance is indirect, as the location serves as a starting point for their journey. The travelers' experiences in Penzance shape their expectations and decisions for the rest of their trip.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SEA-CAVES" target="GROUP">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The group's interaction with John Curgenven and the adjustment of their schedule lead to the successful visit to the sea-caves. This third-order relationship shows how the initial plan, the adjustment, and the final outcome are interconnected, resulting in a positive experience for the group.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SEA-CAVES" target="CORNISHMAN">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Cornish people and the sea-caves is one of caution and respect. The caves are viewed as dangerous and mysterious, and the locals are aware of the risks involved in exploring them. This relationship is shaped by the cultural and historical context of the area, where the sea has always been a source of both opportunity and peril. The Cornish people's understanding of the environment influences their interactions with the caves, highlighting the deep connection between the community and the natural landscape.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LIZARD COAST" target="WRECK OF THE BREST">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The wreck of the Brest had a second-order relationship with the Lizard coast, as it influenced the perception of the area. The wreck made the coast appear 'awful' and highlighted its dangers. This relationship shows how the event shaped the local environment and the experiences of those who lived there.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RAVEN'S UGO" target="SMUGGLING">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The caves, Raven's Ugo and Dolor Ugo, had a historical relationship with smuggling. They were used as hiding places for contraband, which added to their significance in the region. This relationship reflects the caves' role in the local history and their association with illicit activities.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RAVEN'S UGO" target="LOCAL COMMUNITY">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The caves had a second-order relationship with the local community, as they were used for smuggling and later became sites of exploration. This relationship reflects the caves' evolving role in the region, from a place of illicit activity to a site of curiosity and adventure.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-9b862cd9f3d4a2a5394fa477c897d562</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE MISTRESS" target="THE TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The mistress offered to dry the travelers' clothes after they were soaked from walking. This relationship shows her hospitality and willingness to help strangers. It also highlights her role as a laundress and her dedication to maintaining a clean and tidy home. The interaction demonstrates a kind and generous nature, which left the travelers feeling grateful despite their initial inconvenience.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="YOUNG WOMAN" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The young woman allows the travelers to take apples from her tree, showing her generosity and kindness. This relationship is significant as it provides the travelers with food and highlights the hospitality of the Cornish people. The relationship is direct and reflects the cultural values of the region.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HONEST CORNISHMAN" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The honest Cornishman welcomes the travelers into his home and shares his personal history with them. This relationship is significant as it demonstrates his loyalty and devotion to the family he once worked for. The relationship is direct and reflects the deep connections within the Cornish community.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HONEST CORNISHMAN" target="FAMILY">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The honest Cornishman's relationship with the family he once worked for is characterized by loyalty and devotion. This relationship is significant as it shows his strong sense of duty and commitment. The relationship is indirect and reflects the long-term impact of his work and character.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HONEST CORNISHMAN" target="LIGHTHOUSE">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the honest Cornishman and the lighthouse is one of professional achievement and pride. This relationship is significant as it highlights his expertise and dedication. The relationship is direct and reflects his career accomplishments.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="WIFE" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The wife of the honest Cornishman prepares tea for the travelers, showing her hospitality and kindness. This relationship is significant as it reflects the warm and welcoming nature of the Cornish people. The relationship is direct and highlights the importance of family and community in the region.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="WIFE" target="SAILOR">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The sailor tells the story of the wife's survival and eventual death, which had a significant emotional impact on the captain. This relationship demonstrates the deep bond between the couple and the consequences of their ordeal.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LIGHTHOUSE" target="EMPEROR">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The lighthouse is directly connected to the Emperor, who played a pivotal role in its lighting. This relationship is symbolic of the man's achievements and the recognition he received from a higher authority. The Emperor's involvement in the event elevates the lighthouse's significance, linking it to a broader historical context. This relationship highlights the impact of external figures on local events and traditions.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LIGHTHOUSE" target="THE MAN">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The man's relationship with the lighthouse is one of pride and dedication. He is the builder and custodian of the lighthouse, and it holds deep personal and historical significance for him. The lighthouse is not only a physical structure but also a symbol of his achievements and the legacy he wishes to preserve. This relationship underscores the man's connection to his past and his community.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LIGHTHOUSE" target="WAX CANDLE">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The lighthouse is indirectly connected to the Emperor through the wax candle, which was used to light it. This second-order relationship highlights the chain of events that led to the lighthouse's creation and the subsequent preservation of the candle. The candle serves as a bridge between the man and the Emperor, reinforcing the historical and symbolic significance of the lighthouse.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="APPLE TREE" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the apple tree and the travelers is one of sustenance and survival. This relationship is significant as it provides the travelers with food and highlights the natural resources of the Cornish landscape. The relationship is direct and reflects the importance of the environment in the journey.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="BABY" target="CORNISH LANDSCAPE">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the baby and the Cornish landscape is one of innocence and vulnerability. This relationship is significant as it represents the natural beauty and fragility of life. The relationship is indirect and reflects the cultural and environmental context of the region.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c9527d086956d05f8cfb5def3a68555e</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE MAN" target="HIS WIFE">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The man and his wife share a deep, affectionate bond, characterized by mutual support and shared traditions. Their relationship is marked by a sense of partnership and loyalty, as seen in their interactions and the way they carry out daily tasks together. They are described as 'Darby and Joan,' a term that signifies a long-lasting, harmonious marriage. Their relationship is central to the narrative, highlighting the values of love and companionship.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE MAN" target="WAX CANDLE">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The man's relationship with the lighthouse is further reinforced by the annual rituals he performs with the wax candle. These rituals are a direct result of the lighthouse's construction and the Emperor's involvement. The relationship between the man and the lighthouse is thus extended through these traditions, creating a deeper, more enduring connection.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EMPEROR" target="WAX CANDLE">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The wax candle is a direct link to the Emperor and the historical event of the lighthouse's lighting. It serves as a physical representation of the relationship between the man and the Emperor, as well as the man's personal connection to the event. The candle is preserved and honored annually, reinforcing the lasting impact of this relationship on the man's life and traditions.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bc03c3ac54e7909422a954e2a66def93</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GOLDSITHNEY" target="TREVELYANS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of the Trevelyans is closely tied to the rock of Goldsithney. According to the story, an ancestor of the Trevelyans saved himself by swimming his horse to safety when the fair land of Lyonesse was engulfed by the sea. This relationship highlights the historical and mythological significance of the rock and its connection to the Trevelyans.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-369d72e297f786886a6ee02d55512e06</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. AUBYNS" target="NURSERY RHYME">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The St. Aubyns have a second-order relationship with the nursery rhyme, as the rhyme references the town of St. Ives, which is connected to the broader cultural context of the region. This relationship is indirect but still meaningful, as it shows how local folklore and traditions influence the perception of the area. The rhyme adds a layer of cultural depth to the narrative and connects the Mount to other parts of the region.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-744360fec46b02217eb1d4d9f455cb8a</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. AUBYNS" target="YOUNG GUIDE">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The young guide has a second-order relationship with the St. Aubyns, as she is a member of the family and represents their legacy. This relationship is indirect but significant, as it highlights the continuity of the family's presence and influence on the Mount. The guide's actions and demeanor reflect the values and traditions of the St. Aubyns, reinforcing their connection to the location.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-744360fec46b02217eb1d4d9f455cb8a</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. AUBYNS" target="PRINCESS FREDERICA">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the royal party and the St. Aubyns is indirect but significant. The royal party is expected to visit St. Michael's Mount, which is associated with the St. Aubyns. This relationship influences the expectations and preparations of the local community, as well as the potential interactions between the royal party and the St. Aubyns.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="WAITER" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The waiter shows a fatherly interest in the travelers, ensuring they have food and preventing others from taking what belongs to them. This relationship reflects a sense of care and protection, indicating a close and respectful connection between the waiter and the travelers.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CORNISHWOMAN" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The Cornishwoman interacts with the travelers by offering milk and setting a higher price, leading to an unexpected but memorable encounter. This relationship demonstrates a local perspective and the cultural dynamics of the region.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CORNISHWOMAN" target="CORNISH COW">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Cornish cow and the Cornishwoman is direct, as she milks the cow and interacts with the travelers over the sale of milk. This relationship highlights the agricultural aspect of the region and the daily life of the locals.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-bd2b50b24a25833ce136611502f99de5</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. IVES" target="MAN">
  <data key="d5">1.7000000000000002</data>
  <data key="d6">The man has a strong connection to St. Ives, having lived there for 37 years. This is a direct relationship, as his identity and daily life are deeply tied to the town.&lt;SEP&gt;The man's professional background as a shop owner influences his current lifestyle and leisure activities. This is a second-order relationship, as his past career shapes his present behavior and interests.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c07a1f8303c88520a6e687ca76451218</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ST. IVES" target="PRAYER-MEETING">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The man's attendance at the prayer-meeting is influenced by his deep connection to St. Ives. This is a second-order relationship, as his routine and values are shaped by his long-term residence in the town.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-c07a1f8303c88520a6e687ca76451218</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SENNEN" target="CHARACTERS">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the characters and Sennen is one of observation and appreciation. The characters pass through the village and admire its features, indicating that it has a positive impact on their experience. This relationship is direct and reflects the characters' perception of the location.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-1fb3571cbea81efe6dfda2aa2199fdbe</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LIEUTENANT GOLDSMITH" target="LOGAN">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Logan stone and Lieutenant Goldsmith is a direct cause-and-effect relationship. Goldsmith's attempt to move the stone resulted in damage, altering its functionality and creating a lasting impact on the site.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-2ca4427317d91b282aad105e4c1fcb34</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GUIDE" target="VISITORS">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the guide and the visitors involves guidance and safety. The guide ensures that the visitors navigate the dangerous path at Land's End safely, providing support and reassurance. This relationship is essential for the visitors' experience and reflects the guide's professional expertise and care.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PARSON AT ST. SENNEN" target="MR. ----">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the parson at St. Sennen and the long-dead curate of St. Sennen is based on shared history and memory. The parson recalls the curate's visits and his influence on the community. This relationship highlights the continuity of religious and cultural traditions in the area.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SAILOR" target="CAPTAIN">
  <data key="d5">1.5</data>
  <data key="d6">The sailor recounts the story of the captain and his wife, highlighting their survival and the tragic outcome. This relationship shows how the sailor's personal experiences and storytelling influence the narrator's understanding of the area and its history.&lt;SEP&gt;The wife's death and the captain's subsequent mental breakdown are influenced by the sailor's recounting of the event. This third-order relationship shows how the sailor's story impacts the emotional state of the captain and the narrator's reflection on the event.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SAILOR" target="AGAMEMNON">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The sailor's connection to the ship _Agamemnon_ is a direct relationship that shapes his identity and experiences. This relationship reflects the sailor's professional background and the historical context of his service.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-403c2698466591d70033ff7077ada921</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="WHITESAND BAY" target="CHARACTERS">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the characters and Whitesand Bay is one of choice and preference. The characters decide to visit Whitesand Bay instead of other locations, showing that it holds a higher value for them. This relationship is direct and reflects the characters' decision-making process based on their interests.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-1fb3571cbea81efe6dfda2aa2199fdbe</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SHIPWRECK" target="BRISONS">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the shipwreck and the Brisons is direct, as the ship went to pieces near the Brisons. This relationship highlights the historical significance of the Brisons as a site of maritime danger and survival, reinforcing the area's reputation as a place of both beauty and peril.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-b9294464d408e527bddad8101fcc42d3</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="FISHER WIFE" target="SENNEN SCHOOL">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The fisher wife's mention of the Sennen School and her children's academic achievements creates a second-order relationship between the educational institution and the local community. This relationship reflects the importance of education in shaping the lives of the residents and the role of the school in fostering a sense of pride and accomplishment among families.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-a5e39286406a99236327034e4840f578</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="TRISTRAM AND ISEULT" target="LOVE">
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
  <data key="d6">The story of Tristram and Iseult is further connected to the theme of love and betrayal, which is a recurring motif in Arthurian legend. This third-order relationship emphasizes the emotional depth of the tales and how they reflect universal human experiences, even within the fantastical setting of the Arthurian world.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PRINCESS FREDERICA" target="BARON VON PAWEL-RAMMINGEN">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between Princess Frederica and Baron Von Pawel-Rammingen is described as a romantic one, with the latter having loved, wooed, and married the princess. This relationship is central to the narrative, as it explains the reason for their visit to St. Michael's Mount. The bond between them is significant as it influences the actions of the local community and the expectations surrounding their arrival.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-3d113b2b3dce6fe310bc06ea08b364cb</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="TINTAGEL" target="ARTHURIAN LEGEND">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Arthurian legend and the real-world locations like Tintagel and Camelford is a second-order relationship, as it is shaped by the historical and cultural significance of these places. The legends of Arthur are intertwined with the physical locations, creating a connection between myth and reality.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="TINTAGEL" target="ARTHURIAN LEGENDS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the historical Arthurian legends and the modern-day setting of the narrative is one of contrast and reflection. The legends represent an idealized past, while the present-day scenes highlight the mundane and practical aspects of life. This relationship underscores the theme of the enduring nature of ideals despite changing times.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="TINTAGEL" target="CHOUGH">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">Tintagel had a third-order relationship with the chough, as the bird is believed to be the shape of King Arthur's soul and is associated with the ruins of the site. The chough is a symbol of the past and is linked to the myths and legends of Tintagel. This relationship reflects the deep cultural and spiritual connections between the site and the animal.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="UTHER PENDRAGON" target="YGRAYNE">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">Uther Pendragon and Ygrayne were married after Uther killed Ygrayne's husband, the Duke of Cornwall. This relationship is central to the Arthurian legend, as it leads to the birth of Arthur. Their union is a key event in the narrative, setting the stage for Arthur's rise to power and the subsequent events in the legend.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="YGRAYNE" target="KING UTHER PENDRAGON">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">King Uther Pendragon is directly connected to the legend of Ygrayne, as he is the one who is said to have married her after her husband was slain. This relationship highlights the tragic and mythological elements of the Arthurian tales, where the actions of powerful figures have lasting consequences on the lives of others.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ARTHUR" target="MERLIN">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">Merlin played a crucial role in the upbringing of Arthur, ensuring that he was raised in secret and later recognized as the rightful heir to the throne. This relationship is foundational to Arthur's story, as Merlin's guidance and magical interventions shape Arthur's destiny and the course of his reign.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ARTHUR" target="GUINEVERE">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">Arthur and Guinevere were married, but their relationship was strained due to Guinevere's affair with Sir Launcelot. This relationship had a significant impact on Arthur's reign, contributing to the downfall of his kingdom and the eventual fall of Camelot. The betrayal of Guinevere by Launcelot is a pivotal moment in the Arthurian legend.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ARTHUR" target="SIR LAUNCELOT">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">Arthur's relationship with his knights, particularly Sir Launcelot, was central to the Arthurian legend. The betrayal of Launcelot and Guinevere led to the fragmentation of Arthur's court and the eventual collapse of his kingdom. This relationship is a key factor in the tragic end of Arthur's reign.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ARTHUR" target="MORDRED">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between Arthur and Mordred, his nephew, was one of conflict and betrayal. Mordred's rebellion against Arthur led to the final battle at Camelford, where Arthur was mortally wounded. This relationship is a direct cause of Arthur's death and the end of his reign, marking a turning point in the Arthurian legend.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ARTHUR" target="MYTH OF ARTHUR'S DISAPPEARANCE">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between Arthur and the legend of his disappearance is a second-order relationship, as it is influenced by the events of his death and the subsequent myths surrounding his fate. The belief that Arthur will return to restore the Order of Knights of the Round Table is a result of the first-order relationships involving his death and the actions of Sir Bedevere.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ARTHUR" target="TINTAGEL CASTLE">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">Arthur is directly connected to the legend of Tintagel Castle, which is believed to be his birthplace. This relationship highlights the deep historical and mythological significance of the castle in the Arthurian tradition. The legend of Arthur is closely tied to the physical and symbolic presence of the castle in the Cornish landscape.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ARTHUR" target="CORNISH MIND">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of Arthur is indirectly connected to the Cornish people through their belief in the supernatural and mythical elements of the region. This second-order relationship reflects how the stories of Arthur influence the cultural and spiritual practices of the local population, even if they are not directly involved in the events themselves.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="DOZMARE POOL" target="ARTHURIAN LEGEND">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the Arthurian legend and the myth of Dozmare Pool is a second-order relationship, as it is influenced by the events of Arthur's death and the subsequent stories about his disappearance. The pool is a symbolic location in the legend, representing the mystical and supernatural aspects of Arthur's story.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f311566cae6280b6a50b89cb64a60d83</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="TRISTRAM" target="KING MARK">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The legend of Tristram and Iseult is indirectly connected to the broader Arthurian tradition through the involvement of King Mark. This second-order relationship shows how the individual stories of knights and lovers are part of the larger tapestry of Arthurian mythology, where personal dramas intersect with the grand narratives of kings and heroes.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-296767ee0019736ba3b3907d25d663f6</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ISEULT" target="HE">
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the man and Iseult is one of love and loss. The man is deeply affected by his past love, and Iseult's arrival after his death highlights the tragedy of their separation. This relationship is central to the narrative and reflects themes of longing and unfulfilled love.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="BOSSINNEY COVE" target="OLD MAN">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the natural environment and the human activity in the narrative is one of coexistence and interaction. The travelers' journey through the landscape and the old man's work with his donkeys illustrate how humans interact with and depend on the natural world. This relationship emphasizes the harmony and challenges of living in such environments.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="OLD MAN" target="DONKEYS">
  <data key="d5">0.8</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the old man and his donkeys is one of mutual dependence and care. The donkeys are essential to his work, and he treats them with kindness, showing a bond of trust and responsibility. This relationship reflects the simplicity and hard work of rural life.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="OLD MAN" target="TRAVELERS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the travelers and the old man is one of curiosity and interaction. The travelers engage with the old man and his donkeys, highlighting the human connection and the shared experience of the journey. This relationship contributes to the narrative's reflective and observational tone.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-68ddd89fc902a4696967a79c5aea8055</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="FERRABURY CHURCH" target="SHIP">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The ship had a direct relationship with Ferrabury Church, as it was carrying a peal of bells that were intended for the church. The bells were brought to the cove, but the ship sank before they could be delivered. This relationship connects the ship to the church and highlights the tragic loss of the bells and their symbolic significance.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SHIP" target="PILOT">
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
  <data key="d6">The pilot had a direct relationship with the ship, as he was the one who guided it and was the only survivor of the shipwreck. His actions and words influenced the outcome of the journey, leading to the storm and the sinking of the ship. This relationship shows the importance of leadership and the consequences of human decisions.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-f598012485ed9effde140d4a561fd5da</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CHOUGH" target="CORNISHMAN">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the chough and the Cornish people is one of reverence and protection. Due to superstition, the Cornish avoid shooting choughs or ravens, allowing them to live and breed in peace. This relationship is based on cultural beliefs and the perceived spiritual significance of the birds. The chough's survival is directly influenced by this attitude, making it an important part of the local ecosystem. The relationship also reflects the deep connection between the people and their environment.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SLATE QUARRY" target="WORKERS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the slate quarry and the workers is one of labor and danger. The workers face perilous conditions while extracting slate, relying on ropes and their expertise to navigate the cliffs. This relationship is essential for the local economy, as the quarry provides employment and resources. The workers' resilience and adaptability are key aspects of this relationship, reflecting the challenges of their profession. The relationship also underscores the physical and mental demands of the work.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-aa4d7fdb9f89afdae781a33e7abe6b36</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CHARACTERS" target="CLIFFS">
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the characters and the natural landscape, particularly the cliffs and the sea. The characters face challenges while navigating the terrain, which influences their decisions and actions. This relationship reflects the impact of the environment on human activity.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-3cff43d4c0b87d4d234e1ded07f8597b</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CHARACTERS" target="BOATMEN">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the characters and the boatmen, who provide guidance and expertise in navigating the challenging waters. The boatmen's knowledge and experience are crucial for the safety and success of the boat trip. This relationship highlights the importance of local knowledge.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-3cff43d4c0b87d4d234e1ded07f8597b</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CLIFFS" target="GROUP">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the group and the natural environment, including the cliffs, sea, and sky, is significant. The group's experiences on the cliffs and their observations of the sea and sky contribute to the overall atmosphere of the narrative. This relationship highlights the beauty and tranquility of the setting, which plays a key role in the group's journey.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-000278539400b09a416764f3bc60efe5</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GROUP" target="REGRET">
  <data key="d5">0.5</data>
  <data key="d6">The group's decision to go to the sea-caves is influenced by their initial regret over missing the boating excursions. This second-order relationship shows how the group's emotions and previous experiences shape their current decisions and actions.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-94e0080d8e548382ec3fd09c122e9b19</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="VILLAGERS" target="FISHER-MEN">
  <data key="d5">0.6</data>
  <data key="d6">The relationship between the fisher-men and the village is a second-order one, as their presence and activities shape the local economy and daily life. The fisher-men are part of the community that relies on the sea for survival, and their interactions with other villagers, such as the fisher-women, contribute to the social fabric of the village.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-445ffbc070c2c6aec738b62a5960af9d</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="THE TRAVELERS" target="THE LOCALS">
  <data key="d5">0.7</data>
  <data key="d6">The travelers interacted with the locals in Mullion, who spoke in the Cornish dialect. This relationship highlights the cultural diversity and linguistic traditions of the region. The contrast between the locals' speech and the travelers' understanding emphasizes the unique identity of the Cornish people and their long-standing customs.</data>
  <data key="d7">chunk-01da0d7fe4c0b05440c961a17b63f8b1</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
</edge>
</graph></graphml>